WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.110
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:08.170
Life is like a box of chocolates; it’s far
too expensive and I only enjoy about 50% of

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.170
it.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:12.770
But when it comes down to it, what on earth,
or anywhere else, is life?

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:19.670
From our perspective, water and rocks and
spoons and slippers are all inanimate objects

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:24.050
but how do we decide what is alive and what
is not?

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.920
Is there a point where the chemistry suddenly
crosses over?

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:31.010
Would we recognize an alien species if we
saw it?

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:41.100
And if you really really believe hard enough,
can you bring something to life?

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.480
When you’re trying to define life, you realize
that it can’t be a black and white.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.949
It’s like asking how many trees do you need
to make a forest?

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:52.660
What’s the point where a snack becomes a
meal?

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:55.920
And how many cats must you own before you
have a problem?

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.519
There is no one criteria that we can use to
define life and we must remember that we have

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:08.390
an obvious tendency to focus on our planetary
carbon based existence when there are countless

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:14.310
other worlds that could have created living
beings from other elements such as silicon

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.320
or iron or.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:21.640
So if we accept that life is a grey area,
then we must focus on key facets to determine

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.820
if something is alive…

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:27.359
Firstly; complexity, life must be complex.

00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:32.830
Confucius may have said “Life is really
simple, but we insist on making it complicated”

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:36.719
but he often put his pants on backwards and
knew zero about molecular biology.

00:01:36.719 --> 00:01:43.789
Also, life nees to practice homeostasis, meaning
that is has an equilibrium for its internal

00:01:43.789 --> 00:01:50.000
conditions, things like temperature, pH value,
glucose levels, etc.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.130
So what about life on earth?

00:01:53.130 --> 00:02:00.140
What bit of chemical creativity created the
winding path from molecule to monkey to man?

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:06.229
Well, about 4 billion years ago, chemistry
became complicated and reactive enough to

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:08.510
start what we think of as “life”.

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:15.670
The very surprising thing is, this is not
the most important nor most improbable event.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:20.110
You could easily get some form of life if
you had a planet with a similar chemistry

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:25.370
and conditions as ours, and there must be
plenty of these types of planets out there

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.750
in the big bad universe.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:33.510
But on earth, once we had life, it just sat
around as prokaryotes, a very simple form

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.780
of cell, with no nucleus or other complicated
structures inside.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:42.530
As these basic cells, they couldn’t really
change since it was almost impossible to take

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:47.840
in enough energy to make any major upgrade;
it would be like trying to install PS4 games

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.600
on a Gameboy, you’d need a whole room for
the AA batteries alone.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:58.190
So they stayed like that, just little dribbling
morons for about 2 billion years.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:04.620
Then, a very unlikely thing happened, according
to one theory.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:10.260
Archaea are one of the simple cell types that
still exists today in some extreme environments.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:16.660
2 billion years ago, an archae let a bacterium
inside it, possibly through a hole in the

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:17.660
cell wall.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:21.230
And, against all odds, both survived.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.690
The bacteria became what we know as mitochondria.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:29.950
The reason this was so important is that these
mitochondria have an electric charge which,

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.480
for their size, is like a lightning bolt.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.951
It was only with this new pimped out engine
that cells finally had the energy to get more

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:42.210
complex and spawn all the life we see around
us today…

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.030
except squirrels, no-one knows where squirrels
are from.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:50.320
Part of the reason behind this mitochondria
theory is that every living thing shares almost

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:56.520
50% of the same DNA with archaea, a 2-billion-year
old cell.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:02.840
Alright then, if we have a good guess at how
life began, is there anything we can do to

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:07.390
replicate it and start a game of cellular
Sims in a petri dish?

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:11.330
Well we’re actually not too far off getting
life started.

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:17.019
At NASA's Ames Research Center in California,
they set up the best space like conditions

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:21.810
they could, by turning the lights off and
cranking up the air conditioning, well to

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:23.130
put it in simple terms.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:28.460
They then took commonly occurring molecules
of carbon and nitrogen and exposed them to

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:36.569
radiation, managing to produce 3 key chemicals
needed for RNA and DNA, showing that it’s

00:04:36.569 --> 00:04:42.650
possible the components of life could have
come in on an asteroid in the early stages

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.580
of our planet’s development.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.930
Okay, but a bit of space chemistry is hardly
exciting, what about real Frankenstein cells

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:53.070
that we put together from bits of leftover
lunch?

00:04:53.070 --> 00:05:00.590
Well Dr Craig Venter, a pioneer in genetics,
spent 15 years, along with his team, sequencing

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:06.750
the DNA of a bacterium and then artificially
reproducing it, along with a watermark so

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.380
they could track it.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:14.540
They chose Mycoplasma genitalium, the smallest
bacteria in the world, and they injected their

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:19.100
code into an empty cell, after working out
how to rid it of its original DNA.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:25.500
These new artificial cells successfully reproduced,
showing that the process works.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:32.870
But we still don’t really understand these
genitalium.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:36.470
This bacterium has under 500 genes, whereas
humans have 20,000.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:44.290
And of the 500 or so, we’re not sure what
a quarter of them do, aside from the fact

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:45.650
that they are necessary.

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:50.660
Making life is one thing, but controlling
it is a whole other step so it’ll be a while

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:55.530
before you can pop down the pet shop and order
up a monkey with lizard eyes and bat wings.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:06:01.100
Finally, let’s leave boring old reality
behind and have a quick wander into the hypothetical.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:07.900
We’re made of carbon, but what other kinds
of materials could create life and what conditions

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.250
would they need to exist?

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:14.430
Silicon is probably the most likely candidate
since it can create molecules that are large

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:19.300
enough to carry the massive amount of information
you need to encode a lifeform.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:25.510
What’s more, we already have a hint at its
existence here on earth since a type of algae

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:29.550
called diatoms have a cell wall made of silica.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:34.270
Just don’t tell too many people or “carbon-free”
dieting will become a thing.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:41.450
Vadim N. Tsytovich has proposed a possible
space-borne life, in the form of dust suspended

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:42.710
in plasma.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:48.100
In certain extra-terrestrial conditions, the
dust could become charged and begin to self-organize

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:53.621
into complicated structures and from there,
who knows what the dust could evolve into.

00:06:53.621 --> 00:06:59.900
All in all, you are unbelievably lucky to
be alive.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:05.810
It’s not just the peculiar combination of
genes that makes you you, but the overwhelming

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:10.900
amount of chance and good fortune that got
us from little dots in a vast molecular soup,

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:14.449
to big fat walking talking land monkeys.

00:07:14.449 --> 00:07:32.570
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

